# Line endings are part of the bytes, and two things here hash them.
#
# The sealed examinations in tests_solvent/ take the SHA-256 of source
# files with `read_bytes()`, which is the point: a seal that normalised
# what it hashed would not notice a change. On a Windows checkout with
# Git's default `core.autocrlf=true`, every one of those files arrives
# with CRLF, every digest differs, and both exams report the seal
# broken -- five failures that say the code moved when nothing moved.
#
# So the working tree is LF everywhere, on every platform. Windows
# handles LF in source and in text files perfectly well; what it does
# not handle is being told the files are something other than what the
# digests were taken over.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, and must never be translated or diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.whl binary
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf binary

# NSIS reads LF fine. Named rather than left to `text=auto` so that a
# future `.nsi` containing a byte that looks binary is still normalised.
*.nsi text eol=lf
*.nsh text eol=lf
